The issue is clear: when `setScreenLockPin(pinString)` is called on line 65, the `ScreenLockProvider` effect reacts to the `screenLockPin` change and immediately sets `isLocked=true`.

The fix: the provider's cold-start effect should only trigger on mount (initial value from storage), not on every change to `screenLockPin`. After mount, only background→foreground transitions should lock.

Current provider logic:
```ts


useEffect(() => {
  if (!screenLockPin) {
    setIsLocked(false);
    return;
  }
  setIsLocked(true);
}, [screenLockPin, setIsLocked]);
```

This runs on every `screenLockPin` change. When the user sets a new PIN, it immediately locks.

The fix: I'll use a ref to track the initial mount and distinguish between cold-start initialization and subsequent PIN changes. On first mount, I'll set the lock state based on whether a PIN exists. After that, I'll only clear the lock when the PIN is removed, ignoring new PIN assignments.

